  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 PM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nOngoing scattered showers and storms will linger this afternoon\nand early evening in the wake of significant severe weather\nearlier today. This activity is expected to wane over the next\nseveral hours with convective inhibition/subsidence in place\nacross the CWA as the reinforced surface boundary has pushed south\nof the area across central Illinois.\n\nWhile this will likely lead to a lull in shower/storm chances this\nevening, additional thunderstorm development will be possible \novernight tonight -- primarily across northeast Illinois/northwest\nIndiana, including the Chicago area. The aforementioned boundary \nis expected to work back north across the area with time this \nevening with showers/storms possibly training along/north of said \nboundary. Strong buoyancy aloft on the order of 3,500+ MUCAPE \ncombined with deep-layer shear ~35 kts will support a continued \nthreat for strong/severe storms tonight. Given anticipated\nconvective evolution/training of storms, perhaps of equal concern\nwill be the potential for heavy rainfall/flooding later tonight. \nStorms in an environment characterized by ~2\" PWATs would lead to \nvery efficient rainfall, and potentially bring an additional 1-2\"+\nof rain to areas that already saw heavy rainfall earlier in the \nday. While uncertainty remains relatively high at this time \n(20-30% PoPs tonight), another round of impactful storms remains \npossible tonight.\n\nThe aforementioned boundary is expected to remain draped across\nthe area during the day Wednesday with ample instability in place\nalong and to its south, supporting continued thunderstorm chances\nWednesday morning into Wednesday evening. Damaging winds will be\nthe primary threat with strongest storms, especially if another\nline organizes and tracks from northwest to southeast across the\nwarm sector south of the front during the day. Hail and locally \nheavy rainfall will also be possible.\n\nPrecise details in regards to timing and impacts becomes more\nobscure beyond Wednesday, but additional rounds of thunderstorms\nwill be possible into the end of the week as the boundary lingers\nacross the area and keeps a warm, moist airmass in place with\nrelatively strong northwest flow aloft. A more substantial break\nin storm chances may not come until late this upcoming weekend or\nearly next week as surface high pressure may settle more firmly\nover the Midwest.\n\nCornish",
